## Author

Joe Schaefer

## Dedication

To Autumn


## Abstract

Using elementary techniques from Geometric Analysis, Partial Differential Equations, and Abelian $$C^*$$Algebras, we uncover a novel, yet familiar, global geometric discriminant -- namely the indexed set of integrals of triple products of eigenfunctions of the Schr\"odinger operator, to precisely characterize which isospectral Schr\"odinger Operators are isometric.

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} spectrum, Fourier, harmonic, triple products, Schr\"odinger, eigenfunctions, functional, inverse problem, representation theory
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Let $$(M,g)$$ be a smooth, connected, complete Riemannian manifold (possibly non-compact, possibly of infinite volume). Fix a real potential $$V\in C^\infty(M)$$ which is bounded below and proper in the sense

$$V(x)\to +\infty\qquad\text{as }x\to\infty$$

(i.e., $\{x:V(x)\le C\}$ is compact for every $C$). Let

$$H=-\Delta_g+V$$

be the associated Schr\"odinger operator, essentially self-adjoint on $C_c^\infty(M)$. Under these hypotheses it is classical that $$\sigma(H)=\sigma_{\mathrm{disc}}(H)=\{\lambda_0\le\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\}\to+\infty$$, each eigenvalue of finite multiplicity; there is an orthonormal basis $$\{e^i\}_{i=0}^\infty\subset L^2(M,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$$ of smooth eigenfunctions,

$$He^i=\lambda_i e^i.$$

(The same conclusions hold for $$-\Delta_g$$ itself on some complete manifolds of finite volume with no continuous spectrum, but the confining potential makes the analysis cleaner.)
Define the triple products exactly as in the compact case:

$$M^{i,j,k}:=\langle e^i e^j,e^k\rangle_{L^2}=\int_M e^i e^j\,\overline{e^k}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g.$$

Because the eigenfunctions are Schwartz-class at infinity in the sense that they decay faster than any polynomial in $V$ (Agmon estimates [[Agmon1982]](#Agmon1982)/ weighted Sobolev [[TaylorPDE1]](#TaylorPDE1)), the products $$e^i e^j$$ lie in $$L^2$$ and the integrals converge absolutely.

## Results

### Theorem

Let $$(M,g,V)$$ and $$(N,h,W)$$ be two such data, with isospectral Schrödinger operators $$H_M$$ and $$H_N$$. Suppose there exist orthonormal eigenbases $$\{e^i\}$$ of $$H_M$$ and $$\{f^i\}$$ of $$H_N$$, ordered by non-decreasing eigenvalues, such that

```math
M_M^{i,j,k}=M_N^{i,j,k}\qquad\forall\qquad i,j,k.
```
Then there is a diffeomorphism $$F:N\to M$$ with $$F^*g=h$$ and $$V\circ F=W$$.


## Preliminaries

Let $$\mathcal{A}_0\subset L^2(M)$$ be the algebraic span of the eigenfunctions. Finite linear combinations

$$\phi=\sum_{i=0}^N\hat\phi(i)\,e^i$$

are dense in $L^2$ and, by elliptic regularity plus the decay of eigenfunctions, lie in $C^\infty\cap C_0(M)$. Pointwise multiplication is

$$\phi\psi=\sum_k\Bigl(\sum_{i,j}\hat\phi(i)\hat\psi(j)M^{i,j,k}\Bigr)e^k.$$

Thus the structure constants determine the product of any two finite eigenfunction expansions. The same identity holds on $N$. Consequently the linear map

$$\vec F:\mathcal{A}_0(M)\to\mathcal{A}_0(N),\qquad e^i\mapsto f^i$$

is a unital algebra homomorphism for pointwise multiplication (the constant function is a multiple of the ground state only if $V$ is constant; in general $$\vec F$$ need not send $$1$$ to $$1$$, but it preserves the product of any two functions that can be so expanded). Characteristic functions of sublevel sets of $$V$$ can be approximated in $$L^2$$ by functional calculus of $$H$$, so $$\vec F$$ also preserves characteristic functions of sets of finite measure in the $$L^2$$ sense.

This is identical to the compact calculation; the only extra ingredient is that the eigenfunction expansion of a compactly supported smooth function still converges rapidly enough for the triple-product formula to be justified (which follows from the same elliptic estimates that give $$\lambda_i^{N}\|e^i\|_\infty\to 0$$ locally, plus Agmon decay at infinity).

### Proof of Theorem

(Following [[JS24]](#JS24)) For sufficiency, we now consider the linear, bijective orthonormal eigenfunction basis map $$\vec{F}$$ from $$C^\infty(M)$$ to $$C^\infty(N)$$ and note that from the calculations in Preliminaries section above, $$\vec{F}$$ preserves pointwise products for smooth functions (and preserves characteristic functions when extended to $$L^2(M,g)$$) by the premise that $$\set{M^{i,j,k}}$$ is invariant under this map.

#### Lemma

$$\vec F$$ preserves the uniform norm on the span of the eigenfunctions, hence extends to an isometric $$*$$-homomorphism

$$\vec F:C_0(M)\supset\overline{\mathcal{A}_0}^{\|\cdot\|_\infty}\longrightarrow C_0(N).$$

#### Proof of Lemma

On a compact manifold one uses a smooth partition of unity and the fact that $$\|a\|_\infty=1$$ for a partition function $a$ is detected by $$\|a^p\|_\infty=1$$ for all $$p$$, which is visible in the Fourier coefficients because the limiting characteristic function of $$\{a=1\}$$ has $$L^2$$-mass.

In the non-compact setting replace a global partition of unity by a sequence of cutoffs $$\chi_R$$ that equal $$1$$ on $$\{V\le R\}$$ and vanish outside $$\{V\le R+1\}$$. On each such “almost-compact” region the same argument applies: if $$\|\phi\|_\infty=c$$ is attained on a set of positive measure inside $$\{V\le R\}$$, the powers

$$\phi^p/\|\phi\|_\infty^p$$

converge in $$L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}$$ to a characteristic function, and $$\vec F$$ preserves that limit because it preserves all triple products. Sending $$R\to\infty$$ and using that every function in $$C_0(M)$$ is uniformly small outside a large sublevel set of $$V$$ gives $$\|\vec F(\phi)\|_\infty=\|\phi\|_\infty$$.

\end{subproof}

Now we apply the Gelfand-Naimark-Segal Representation Theorem (in contravariant functor form) for Abelian $$C^*$$ algebras $$C_0(N), C_0(M)$$ [[JC19]](#JC19) to represent this isomorphism $$\vec{F}$$ by a homeomorphism $$F$$ between $$N$$ and $$M$$. Since $$\vec{F}$$ is bijective on smooth functions, $$F$$ too must be smooth.

As this now diffeomorphism preserves eigenvalues and eigenfunctions (by hypothesis on $$\vec{F}(f) = f \circ F$$), it must preserve the Schr\"odinger operator on smooth functions. By construction

$$\vec F(\phi)=\phi\circ F$$

on the eigenbasis, hence on all finite combinations. Therefore

$$H_N(\phi\circ F)=(H_M\phi)\circ F$$

for every eigenfunction $$\phi$$, and by density for every Schwartz-class function. In particular $$F$$ pulls eigenfunctions of $$H_M$$ back to eigenfunctions of $$H_N$$ with the same eigenvalues.

Writing $$H=-\Delta+V$$ in local coordinates, the principal symbol of $$H$$ is the same as that of $$-\Delta$$, namely the cometric $$g^{ij}\xi_i\xi_j$$. An elliptic operator that is intertwined by a diffeomorphism has its principal symbol pulled back. Hence

$$F^*g=h.$$

The zeroth-order terms then give $$V\circ F=W$$.

This completes the proof of the Theorem.



## Example
On $$M=\mathbb{R}$$ with the Euclidean metric, take

$$H=-\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+x^2.$$

The potential $$V(x)=x^2$$ is smooth, bounded below, and $$V(x)\to+\infty$$ as $$|x|\to\infty$$. Hence $$\sigma_{\mathrm{ess}}(H)=\emptyset$$. The spectrum is

$$\lambda_n=2n+1,\qquad n=0,1,2,\dots$$

(each of multiplicity one). The corresponding $$L^2(\mathbb{R})$$-normalized eigenfunctions are the Hermite functions

$$\psi_n(x)=\bigl(2^n n!\,\sqrt{\pi}\bigr)^{-1/2}H_n(x)\,e^{-x^2/2},$$

where $$H_n$$ are the physicist’s Hermite polynomials. They form an orthonormal basis of $$L^2(\mathbb{R})$$, lie in the Schwartz class $$\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})\subset C_0(\mathbb{R})$$, and satisfy the Agmon decay that makes every pointwise product $$\psi_i\psi_j$$ square-integrable.

### Triple Products

Because the eigenfunctions are real,

$$M^{i,j,k}=\langle\psi_i\psi_j,\psi_k\rangle=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\psi_i(x)\psi_j(x)\psi_k(x)\,dx.$$

These integrals vanish unless $$i+j+k$$ is even (parity) and the three indices satisfy a triangle-type constraint coming from the linearization of Hermite polynomials. The linearization formula

$$H_n(x)H_m(x)=\sum_{r=0}^{\min(n,m)}\binom{n}{r}\binom{m}{r}r!\,2^r\,H_{n+m-2r}(x)$$

together with the Gaussian weight converts, after normalizing, into an explicit formula for $$M^{i,j,k}$$. Equivalently, the generating-function identity

$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-x^2}H_a(x)H_b(x)H_c(x)\,dx
=\sqrt{\pi}\,2^N\frac{a!\,b!\,c!}{(N-a)!(N-b)!(N-c)!}$$

(when $$a+b+c=2N$$ is even and each factorial in the denominator is defined and non-negative, and $$0$$ otherwise) yields $$M^{i,j,k}$$ after inserting the normalization constants of the $$\psi_n$$. In particular:

$$M^{n,n,0}=\int\psi_n^2\psi_0\,dx>0$$ (the ground state $$\psi_0=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-x^2/2}$$ is a strictly positive Gaussian);
the map $$\psi_i\psi_j=\sum_k M^{i,j,k}\psi_k$$ is the exact multiplication rule in the Hermite basis.

So the structure constants of pointwise multiplication on the dense subspace $$\mathrm{span}\{\psi_n\}\subset C_0(\mathbb{R})$$ are known in closed form.

### What the theorem says

Suppose $$(\mathbb{R},g,W)$$ is another complete Riemannian line (so $$g=e^{2\phi(x)}\,dx^2$$ in some coordinate) with a smooth confining potential $$W$$, and suppose its Schrödinger operator $$-\Delta_g+W$$ is isospectral to $$H$$ and admits an eigenbasis $$\{f_n\}$$ with the same triple products $$\langle f_i f_j,f_k\rangle=M^{i,j,k}.$$

The discrete-spectrum argument then produces a diffeomorphism  $$F:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$$ such that

$$F^*g=dx^2,\qquad W\circ F=x^2.$$

In one dimension this is almost tautological once the algebra is identified, but the mechanism is visible by hand:

Finite Hermite combinations are dense in $$C_0(\mathbb{R})$$ for the uniform norm (Stone–Weierstrass on each compact plus Gaussian decay at infinity). The numbers $$M^{i,j,k}$$ determine the product of any two such combinations, hence determine a $$C^*$$-isomorphism

$$C_0(\mathbb{R})\longrightarrow C_0(\mathbb{R}),\qquad \psi_n\mapsto f_n.$$

Gelfand–Naimark supplies a homeomorphism of the underlying locally compact spaces; smoothness of the eigenfunctions upgrades it to a diffeomorphism $$F$$. Intertwining of $$H$$ forces the principal symbol (the cometric) and the zeroth-order term (the potential) to match.

Because every eigenspace is one-dimensional, the singular-value packaging collapses to the absolute values $$|M^{i,j,k}|$$ together with a consistent choice of signs (the “diagonal litmus” of the compact paper: the products $$M^{i,i,k}$$ determine the even part of the algebra and fix most signs).

### Explicit Formula

$$M^{i,j,k}=\langle\psi_i\psi_j,\psi_k\rangle
=\int_{\mathbb{R}}\psi_i(x)\psi_j(x)\psi_k(x)\,dx
=N_i N_j N_k\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_i(x)H_j(x)H_k(x)\,e^{-3x^2/2}\,dx,$$

where $$N_n=(2^n n!\sqrt{\pi})^{-1/2}$$.

$$M^{i,j,k}=0$$ unless both of the following hold -- parity: $$i+j+k$$ is even; triangle: $$|i-j|\le k\le i+j$$ (and cyclic permutations).

These are the oscillator analogues of the Clebsch–Gordan support conditions.

Change variables $$x=\sqrt{2/3}\,y$$. Then

$$\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_i(x)H_j(x)H_k(x)\,e^{-3x^2/2}\,dx
=\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_i\Bigl(\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,y\Bigr)
H_j\Bigl(\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,y\Bigr)
H_k\Bigl(\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,y\Bigr)\,e^{-y^2}\,dy.$$

Each scaled polynomial expands by the finite identity

$$H_n(\gamma y)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\gamma^{n-2\ell}(\gamma^2-1)^\ell\binom{n}{2\ell}\frac{(2\ell)!}{\ell!}\,H_{n-2\ell}(y),$$

with $$\gamma=\sqrt{2/3}$$. The product of the three expansions is a finite linear combination of products $$H_a H_b H_c$$. Titchmarsh’s formula then evaluates every remaining integral:

```math
\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_a H_b H_c\,e^{-y^2}\,dy
=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{\sqrt{\pi}\,2^{s}\,a!\,b!\,c!}{(s-a)!(s-b)!(s-c)!}
& \text{if }a+b+c=2s\text{ is even and }s\ge a,b,c,\\
0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
```

The result is a finite triple sum (at most $O(ijk)$ terms, typically far fewer). Combining the prefactors $$N_i N_j N_k\sqrt{2/3}$$ gives $$M^{i,j,k}$$ exactly.

Equivalently, the product of two normalized functions expands in a scaled Hermite basis

$$\psi_i(x)\psi_j(x)=\sum_{r=0}^{\lfloor(i+j)/2\rfloor}c_{r;ij}\,\psi_{i+j-2r}(\sqrt{2}\,x),$$

with explicit coefficients

$$c_{r;ij}
=\frac{(-1)^r}{\sqrt{2^{i+j-2r+1}\pi}}
\sqrt{\frac{(i+j-2r)!}{i!\,j!}}
\binom{i}{r}\binom{j}{r}r!\,2^{r}$$

(up to the conventional placement of the $$\sqrt{2}$$ in the argument; cf. [[K16]](##K16)). Then

$$M^{i,j,k}=\sum_{r}c_{r;ij}\int_{\mathbb{R}}\psi_{i+j-2r}(\sqrt{2}\,x)\,\psi_k(x)\,dx,$$

and each scaled overlap is a $${}_2F_1$$ (or terminates as a short hypergeometric sum). This is the form best suited to computation.

